# ---------------------------------------------------------------- stroke volume, preload, afterload
B.concept("phys-starling", "Frank-Starling law, preload and afterload", [
    "Frank-Starling law: force of contraction is proportional to the initial length of the cardiac muscle fiber (EDV and stroke volume).",
    "Resting cardiac fiber length is less than optimum, so more filling stretches fibers closer to optimum and increases the force of the next contraction.",
    "Preload = load before contraction, the end-diastolic volume. Afterload = load the ventricle pumps against, the arterial blood pressure.",
    "Sympathetic stimulation and epinephrine increase contractility at any EDV (more Ca2+ influx) and shift the Frank-Starling curve to the left.",
], L8)

B.mc("A client receives a fluid bolus that increases end-diastolic volume, and the stroke volume rises. Which mechanism explains the stronger contraction?",
     "Greater stretch brings cardiac fibers closer to their optimum length, so contraction force increases.",
     [("Sympathetic stimulation shifts the Frank-Starling curve to the left.", "That is extrinsic control, which raises contractility at any EDV; the bolus works through intrinsic control.", "confuses intrinsic and extrinsic control"),
      ("Afterload increases, forcing the ventricle to contract harder.", "Afterload is arterial pressure; a fluid bolus acts on preload (EDV).", "mixes up preload and afterload"),
      ("Parasympathetic stimulation increases ventricular contractility.", "Parasympathetic stimulation has no effect on ventricular muscle.")],
     "This is the Frank-Starling law, the intrinsic control of stroke volume. Resting cardiac fiber length is shorter than the optimum length for maximum tension. Extra filling raises EDV, stretches the fibers closer to optimum and so increases the force of the next contraction, raising stroke volume. The extent of filling is the preload.",
     L8)

B.tf("A nurse defines afterload as the end-diastolic volume that stretches the ventricle before it contracts.", False,
     "False. That definition is preload (the load before contraction, the end-diastolic volume). Afterload is the load against which the ventricle pumps, i.e. the pressure in the artery (arterial blood pressure).",
     L8, "End-diastolic volume is preload; afterload is arterial pressure.", "mixes up preload and afterload")

